<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest — required for App Store submission.

  DRAFT, 2026-08-24. Derived from what the code actually does, not from the
  privacy policy (which is itself still a draft with open TODOs). Verify each
  block against the App Store Connect privacy questionnaire before submitting;
  the two must agree, and this file is the one Apple diffs against the SDKs.

  Sources for each declaration:
    Health / Fitness   HealthKit reads -> training_logs, daily_biometrics
                       (resting HR, sleep, HRV). NSHealthShareUsageDescription.
    Precise location   per-second GPS in workout streams -> external_streams.
                       Start/end points are NOT trimmed, so this is
                       home-address precision. NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription.
    Audio              voice memos -> training-memos bucket (private, owner-
                       folder scoped). NSMicrophoneUsageDescription.
    Email / Name       Supabase Auth account + athlete profile.
    User ID            auth.uid(), the key every row is scoped by.
    Crash / Perf       sentry-cocoa. The SDK ships its own manifest; this
                       declares the app's own collection, as Apple requires.
    Product inter.     which screens and features are used.

  NOT declared, deliberately — confirm these stay true:
    * No tracking. No advertising SDK, no data broker, no cross-app linkage.
      NSPrivacyTracking is false and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is empty.
    * No file-timestamp required-reason API. VideoDownloadManager calls
      attributesOfItem but reads only .size, which is not a covered API.
    * No contacts, photos, search history, purchases, or sensitive info
      categories.

  OPEN QUESTION for the submission: data sent to third-party AI providers for
  coaching responses includes HealthKit-derived values. App Review 5.1.3
  requires explicit user consent for sharing HealthKit data with third
  parties, and the app does not currently ask for it separately from the
  HealthKit permission itself. That consent flow is a blocker independent of
  this file.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>

    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
